WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.804
Cedar Crossing is considering three options.

2
00:00:03.004 --> 00:00:05.464
Keep the room as it is and improve signs.

3
00:00:05.664 --> 00:00:08.459
Pilot scheduled quiet and group-use periods.

4
00:00:08.659 --> 00:00:10.583
Or pursue a major renovation.

5
00:00:10.783 --> 00:00:12.476
None is automatically right.

6
00:00:12.676 --> 00:00:17.895
Define the problem first: people need suitable space,
not necessarily a new building.

7
00:00:18.095 --> 00:00:21.129
Next, set criteria before judging the options.

8
00:00:21.329 --> 00:00:27.445
Our comparison considers user needs, accessibility,
staff capacity, cost, and flexibility.

9
00:00:27.645 --> 00:00:32.260
Clearer signs have a low initial cost but do not resolve
competing uses.

10
00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:37.020
A schedule pilot is reversible, but needs communication
and staff attention.

11
00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:45.360
Renovation may offer greater separation, but requires
funding, specialist design, disruption planning, and
continuing support.

12
00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:51.973
For this fictional story, the manager selects the
schedule pilot to gather more evidence within current
capacity.

13
00:00:52.173 --> 00:00:55.988
That is a conditional choice, not a universal
recommendation.

14
00:00:56.188 --> 00:01:00.264
Groups can bring different knowledge and increase
understanding of a decision.

15
00:01:00.464 --> 00:01:03.545
They also take time and may feel pressure to agree.

16
00:01:03.745 --> 00:01:07.168
Clarify who advises, who decides, and who implements.

17
00:01:07.368 --> 00:01:11.532
Even a numerical comparison involves judgments about
criteria.

18
00:01:11.732 --> 00:01:15.347
After implementation, review whether the choice actually
helped.

